# Porous Organic Cage Induced Spontaneous Restructuring of Buried Interface Toward High‐Performance Perovskite Photovoltaic

## abstract
In perovskite solar cells (PSCs), the buried interface containing high concentrations of defects is critical for efficient charge extraction toward high‐performance device. Herein, porous organic cage (POC) is introduced between tin dioxide and perovskite to spontaneously reconstitute the buried interface. Through the chemical linkage formed by polydentate chelation of POC with SnO2 and perovskite, the buried interface achieves greatly reduced defect density and enhanced carrier extraction. More importantly, it is found that iodide ions in aged devices to migrate down to the electron transport layer and even invade the ITO electrode, changing the work function of ITO. This detrimental effect can be well resolved by POC since the host‐guest interaction of POC can effectively suppress the iodide ions trying to migrate downward. As a result, the PSC fabricated by POC‐restructured strategy yields a superior PCE of 24.13%. Moreover, the unencapsulated PSCs exhibit conspicuous improved long‐term stability and retain 93% of its initial efficiency after 5000 h in ambient condition.

In perovskite solar cells (PSCs), the buried interface containing high concentrations of defects is critical for efficient charge extraction toward high-performance device. Herein, porous organic cage (POC) is introduced between tin dioxide and perovskite to spontaneously reconstitute the buried interface. Through the chemical linkage formed by polydentate chelation of POC with $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ and perovskite, the buried interface achieves greatly reduced defect density and enhanced carrier extraction. More importantly, it is found that iodide ions in aged devices to migrate down to the electron transport layer and even invade the ITO electrode, changing the work function of ITO. This detrimental effect can be well resolved by POC since the host-guest interaction of POC can effectively suppress the iodide ions trying to migrate downward. As a result, the PSC fabricated by POC-restructured strategy yields a superior PCE of $24.73\%$ . Moreover, the unencapsulated PSCs exhibit conspicuous improved long-term stability and retain $93\%$ of its initial efficiency after $5000\textmd{h}$ in ambient condition.
## 1. Introduction
Photovoltaic technologies, as low-cost and environmentally friendly energy source, are considered to be an indispensable force in solving the energy crisis and achieving carbon neutrality in this century.[1] Among emergent solar cells, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are regarded as the most promising candidates for photovoltaic applications by both academia and industry, due to facial fabrication processing, earth-abundant constituents, outstanding optoelectronic properties and potential low energy consumption.[2–4] Recently, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs has upgraded to $25.7\%$ , capable to rival mature silicon solar cells.[5] The final implementation of this photovoltaic technology relies on the balance between high efficiency and long-term stability, which is the focus of current academic research.[6–8]
Typically, PSCs are heterojunction configuration composed of perovskite layer sandwiched between carrier transport layers (CTLs): electron transport layer (ETL) and hole transport layer (HTL). With sufficient diffusion lengths in perovskite bulk,[9] non-radiative recombination is more likely to occur at the interface between CTLs and perovskite.[10,11] The crystal growth fracture enriches high trap states at the heterointerface, forming the center of interface recombination,[12–14] which hinders the increase of the open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ and fill factor (FF) of PSCs.[15] Therefore, intensive research efforts have been dedicated to defect passivation, in which organic long-chain amine salts, perovskite quantum dots, and 2D materials have achieved promising results in suppressing specific defects at upper surface (HTL and perovskite) and grain boundaries.[14,17–20] Significant progress has been made in device performance by post-treatment of the upper surface of perovskite film in n-i-p architecture solar cells. On the contrary, the research on the buried interface (ETL and perovskite) remains in its infancy and needs to be developed urgently.
Actually, the buried interface is crucial for the efficiency and stability of PSCs.[8,13,21] Although $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is considered to be an ideal electron transport material in n-i-p PSCs due to its fast electron mobility and favorable conduction band alignment,[22,23] a large number of O vacancies $(V_{\mathrm{O}})$ and $\mathrm{sn}$ interstitials $\left(\mathrm{{Sn}_{i}\right)}$ have been reported to exist on the surface of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ prepared by low temperature method.[23,24] Inorganic compounds $\mathrm{\Gamma(HCOONH{_{4}},}$ $\mathsf{N b C l}_{5}$ , etc.) and organic molecules with appropriated functional groups have been adopted to suppress the formation of surface defects.[24–31] In addition, there are also I vacancies $(\mathrm{V_{I}})$ , zero-valent lead $({\mathrm{Pb}}^{0})$ and antisite defects $(\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}})$ at the bottom interface of perovskite film.[12,32,33] Huang’s report revealed that the concentration of deep level defects at the buried interface are even higher than the upper surface.[11] To settle this problem, ammonium salts or molecules containing specific groups were doped into the precursor solution to achieve passivation of the perovskite bottom.[15,33] However, the research on tailoring the buried interface is still challenging due to the constraints of the layer-by-layer solution process.[34] The massive defects at the buried interface is not only unfavorable for electron extraction, but also affects the growth of perovskite layer.[33,35] Therefore, it is urgent to explore a strategy capable to manage the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -perovskite buried interface, thereby drastically minimizing the nonradiative recombination pathway and facilitating charge extraction.
Another major problem that has long plagued PSCs is the poor stability.[35] As ionically bonded semiconductors, the softness of the lattice leads to a unique phenomenon in perovskite: ion migration.[37] Worse still, the unavoidable external factors such as illumination, electric field, humidity and heat in the actual operating state further enhance the characteristics of ion migration.[38,39] Organic cations and halide anions with low migration activation energy in the perovskite component, especially iodide ions, are extremely prone to form vacancies and migrate in the lattice.[40] Severe ion migration can cause stoichiometric imbalance, element redistribution, and deeplevel defects, and eventually trigger a chain reaction to cause irreversible decomposition of perovskite, which seriously restricts the lifetime of the device.[37] Although ion migration is fundamentally important in perovskite photovoltaic community, research on ion migration toward buried interface or even substrate interface (interface between the conductive transparent substrate electrode such as indium tin oxide (ITO) and ETL) remained largely unexplored.[26,41] Therefore, it is highly required to deeply investigate such downward ion migration, its effect on the photovoltaic performance of PSCs and develop strategies for inhibiting downward ion migration to enhance the intrinsic stability of PSCs.
As a subclass of organic porous solid materials, porous organic cages (POCs) are constructed from discrete organic molecules with intrinsic cavities through weak intermolecular interaction. POC possesses good electronic and ionic conductivity like other porous organic materials.[42] Different from most metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent-organic frameworks (COFs), which are infinite extended and insoluble frameworks linked by strong coordination and covalent bonds, the POCs possess excellent discrete property.[42] The discrete property of POCs makes them soluble in different solvents $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , DMF, DMSO, etc.),[44] which would be very favorable to be integrated into PSCs prepared by solution processing methods. In addition, with a large number of chelation sites, POCs can anchor different types of ions and molecules through coordination, hydrogen bonding, as well as host-guest interactions, etc.[42] These unique features enable them to be widely applied in important fields such as drug delivery, gas adsorption, sewage treatment, and energy storage.[44,45] However, to date, exploiting POCs as interfacial functional materials for PSCs have not been reported yet.
In this work, we found that the buried interface of PSCs can be finely tailored by soluble POC. On the one hand, like threading a needle, the abundant functional groups on POC provide substantial chelating sites to chemically connect $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite, stitching the buried interface into a monolith and achieving spontaneous restructuring. In this way, diverse defects on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface and the bottom of the perovskite are simultaneously manipulated by polydentate chelation, thereby greatly reducing the nonradiative recombination sites and promoting the crystallization of the perovskite (Figure  1a–c). On the other hand, the host-guest interaction of POC can effectively clamp iodide ions and prevent the migrated iodide ions from invading the buried interface and ITO (Figure  1b). As a result, the POC-restructured strategy boosted the PCE of PSCs to $24.13\%$ . Meanwhile, the device exhibited conspicuous improved long-term stability, retaining $93\%$ of the initial PCE after $5000\mathrm{{h}}$ in ambient condition. Overall, the multi-functional synergistic effects of POC to the buried interface offer new insights on restructuring interface by porous organic materials toward high-performance perovskite photovoltaics.
## 2. Results and Discussion
As shown in Figure 1a,b, the buried interface of the n-i-p PSC with planar structure was tailored by hydrazone-linked porous organic cage (POC) by spin coating POC solution onto $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface during PSC fabrication. Figure  1c shows the chemical composition of POC, which is a lantern-like cage formed by the self-assembly of calix[4] resorcinarene (C4RA) and succinic dihydrazide (SDH) through a hydrazone bonds.[42] The two ends of SDH contain abundant polydentate chelating sites to coordinate with metal ions, while the outward-facing tert-butyl group of the C4RA imparts strong hydrophobicity. In addition, there is a cavity with a height of ${\approx}1.4\mathrm{nm}$ and volume of ${\mathrm{\tilde{\tau}}}_{\approx650\mathrm{\AA}^{3}}$ inside the POC molecule, which can capture some small molecules or ions (like gas molecules or radioactive iodine ions, etc.) specifically through host-guest interaction (Figure 1b).[42]
## 2.1. Interaction between POC and $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$
Atomic force microscopy (AFM) was first performed to observe the effect of POC treatment on surface topography of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . As shown in Figure  2a,b, the surface roughness is significantly reduced from 5.9 to $1.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ with no obvious change in the distribution of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles. This phenomenon can be also found in larger scale (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The smoother surface could facilitate subsequent deposition of highquality perovskite film with good interfacial contact.[35] In addition, the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns show that the average grain size of the $\mathsf{P O C}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ films (POC spin coated onto $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ) does not change significantly (Figure 2c). The optical transmission spectra of both pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{POC}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film exhibit excellent transmittance in the visible spectrum (Figure  2d), indicating that POC has little effect on the light absorption of the perovskite layer.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted to uncover the interaction between POC and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , and the results are depicted in Figure $2\mathrm{e-g}$ and Figure  S2 (Supporting Information). In the N1s high resolution spectrum (Figure  2e), a peak at $400.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ appears in the pure POC sample, but not in the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sample. The same peak also appears in POC$\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sample, confirming the existence of POC on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface. Furthermore, the N1s peak at $400.0\ \mathrm{eV};$ corresponding to the $N$ from amine group, becomes weaker and slightly blueshifts toward higher binding energy in $\mathrm{POC}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sample compared to POC sample.[33] Correspondingly, the 3d peaks of $\mathrm{sn}$ are slightly red-shifted from 495.2 $(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2})$ and $486.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ $(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})$ to 494.8 and $486.4\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, in the $\mathrm{POC}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sample (Figure 2f ). The shift of $N$ and $\mathrm{{Sn}}$ peak positions may be attributed to the electrostatic coupling between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and amine groups in POC.[28,35] As presented in Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ , an asymmetric spectral profile can be observed in the O1s spectra of the initial and modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film. The O1s peak is further deconvoluted into two peaks, the stronger peak at $530.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ originates from $\mathrm{{Sn-O-Sn}}$ in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{O_{L}})$ , and the other peak at $531.5\mathrm{eV}$ belongs to surface oxygen vacancies or adsorbed hydroxyl groups $(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{v}})$ .[24,35] After POC treatment, the binding energy of the peak of O1s becomes slightly larger, indicating a change in the valence state of oxygen. The peak area ratio $(S_{\mathrm{OV}}/(S_{\mathrm{OV}}/+S_{\mathrm{OL}}))$ of $\mathsf{P O C}.\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ is $28.6\%$ , lower than $31.9\%$ of the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . This result implies that the oxygen vacancies on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface are effectively filled by electron-rich carbonyl groups in POC.
To further explore the mechanism of interaction between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and POC, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was carried out. Figure 2h,i clearly show the absorption peaks in POC, corresponding to $_\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching $(3337~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ ), ${\tt N}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{H}}$ bending $(1518~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching $(1676~\mathrm{~cm}^{-1})$ .[42,46] When POC was deposited on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, these three peaks were shifted down to 3326, 1499, and $1661\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. It is reasonable to speculate that the above downshifts are caused by the interaction between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and the specific group of POC. In general, POC can be connected with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ through electrostatic coupling and coordination (Figure  1b), spontaneously restructuring the surface on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ side and potentially leading to the reduction of surface defect density.
## 2.2. Interaction between POC and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$
The carbonyl and amine groups in POC can not only interact with $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , but also complex with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in perovskite, which will be verified by the following series of characterization methods. As shown in Figure 3a, the peak of $\mathrm{Pb}$ 4f in the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ film treated by POC $(143.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $138.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2})$ is shifted to lower binding energy compared with the pristine $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ $143.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $138.8~\mathrm{eV}$ for $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\backslash$ ), suggesting that Pb gain electrons from lone pair electrons of the carbonyl group on POC.[37] Besides, the peak of I 3d is red-shifted, suggesting that the amine group in the POC may form hydrogen bond with iodine (Figure S3, Supporting Information).[28,35]
To evaluate the interaction between POC and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , Tyndall effect based on precursor colloid scattering was employed (Figure  3b).[47] First, $1.5\mathrm{~s~}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ was directly dissolved in pure DMF, and the resulting solution was turbid with disordered light scattering, implying the existence of a large number of Pb-I clusters of different scales. Then, the solution mixed with $5\%$ DMSO became clearer with less light scattering, but there was still some amount of precipitation. After the subsequent addition of POC, the solution became much clearer and an obvious light path appeared. The clearest solution and weakest light scattering indicated that the coordination between POC and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ greatly reduced the size and disorder of the $\mathrm{Pb\mathrm{-}I}$ clusters.[47] In addition, the diffraction peak of (001) is significantly enhanced in $\mathsf{P O C}\mathrm{.PbI}_{2}$ sample (Figure  S4, Supporting Information), signifying that such an interaction could promote the crystallinity of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ films, which is more favorable for subsequent perovskite growth.
FTIR and liquid-state nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) were conducted to further elucidate the POC and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ interaction at molecular level. As clearly observed in Figure 3c,d, both IR absorption peaks of the hydroxyl group $(3337~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , the amine group $(1518~\mathrm{cm^{-1}},$ ), and the carbonyl group $(1676~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ tend to shift to lower wavenumbers in $\mathrm{POC}{\cdot}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ sample, apparently attributed to the coordination of these groups with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ The liquid NMR spectrum further gives more precise evidence for the interaction between POC and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ .[28,42] According to the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR in Figure 3e, the proton resonance signals of Ha and Hb at 8.52 and $11.78\ \mathrm{ppm}$ are derived from -NH- near the carbonyl group and $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathrm{H}\mathbf{\cdotC}=\mathrm{N}.$ at the tail in POC, respectively. With the addition of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ to the POC solution, the peak of Ha shifted from 8.52 to $8.55~\mathrm{ppm}$ , and Hb peak shifted from 11.78 to $11.83~\mathrm{ppm}$ . Given the absence of H atoms on the carbonyl group, $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR was applied to examine the interaction between the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ .[48] Clearly, the C peak corresponding to the carbonyl group in POC moved from 162.77 to $162.80~\mathrm{ppm}$ after the addition of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (Figure  3f). According to the results of NMR and FTIR, it can be deduced that the carbonyl and amine groups in POC constitute the polydentate O-N-O chelating site with the ability to complex with lead ions (Figure  S5, Supporting Information).[42] With the strong co­ordination with metal ions, POC seems to build a bridge between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite layer, reducing the adverse effect of double sides in buried interface. This is a quite unique feature and fulfills a spontaneous restructuring of buried interface via porous organic cage through its coordination with $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , which could substantially reduce interfacial defect at buried interface. In addition, the results of time of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (ToF-SIMS) showed that most of the POC molecules remained at the buried interface after the formation of the PSC (Figure S6, Supporting Information).
To further assess the effect of POC on perovskite, the perovskite films prepared on glass/ $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ were characterized by XRD (Figure  3g). The control perovskite film exhibits a very strong $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ signal $(12.6^{\circ})$ . In contrast, the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ signal of the POC-tailoring films was observably attenuated (Figure S7, Supporting Information). This phenomenon can also be visually demonstrated by the SEM images of the POC-perovskite film (Figure  3h,i). Another impressive feature of XRD is that the (111) facet orientation is suppressed and the (100) facet orientation is markedly promoted by POC treatment (Figure  3g).[49] Theoretical calculations show that the defect density of perovskites is facet-dependent, with the highest defect density appearing on the (111) facet, whereas the (100) facet tend to have the fewest defects.[50] Therefore, promoting the dominance of the favorable (100) facet orientation may benefit for reducing the defect density and improve the crystal quality of perovskite film.[49] Such high crystal quality promotes a twofold increase in the charge nonradiative recombination lifetime from 0.97 to $1.9~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ of the POC-treated perovskite film, and the emission peak of the steady-state PL is also enhanced, as shown in photoluminescence spectra (Figure  S8 and Table  S1, Supporting Information).
## 2.3. Reduced Trap-State Density and Enhanced Charge Extraction
Efficient perovskite solar cells rely on superior carrier extraction and transport, which requires low defect density at the interface.[8] In order to detect the electronic defect density $(N_{\mathrm{t}})$ at the buried interface, the space-charge-limited current (SCLC) method was used on electron-only devices, as depicted in Figure 4a,b. Through reconstructing buried interface, the trapfilled-limit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ of the POC-device dropped from 0.238 to 0.101 V. Therefore, the calculated density of the target device $(1.12\times10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ reduced by more than one-half compared to the control device $(2.64\times10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ . Next, electrical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was carried out to probe the defect state distribution in real PSCs under illumination. Figure $4\mathrm{c}$ shows that the trap density of states (tDOS) is greatly reduced at almost the full energy-level depth, especially in the deeper band.[49] Furthermore, the Nyquist plot reveals that the POC-device has a fitted composite resistance of $275\ \Omega$ , obviously higher than $98\Omega$ of the control device (Figure 4d), representing suppressed non-radiative recombination.[51] Evidently, the spontaneously restructured buried interface enables a substantial reduction in the defect density within the device level.
Upon the coordination between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and POC, significant changes in the band structure of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ were observed on UV photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) after POC treatment. The cutoff edge and onset value of the binding energy are shown in Figure  S9 (Supporting Information), and the extracted Fermi level and valence band maximum are listed in Table S2 (Supporting Information). The optical bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}}=3.89\ \mathrm{eV}$ ) of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is obtained from the Tauc plot and is not affected by POC (Figure  S10, Supporting Information).[28] According to the above information, the energy level diagram of layers in PSCs can be plotted as shown in Figure  S11 (Supporting Information). The conduction band minimum of $\mathrm{POC}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is raised from $-4.19$ to $-4.05\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Such enhanced energy level matching is more favorable for efficient electron extraction. Then, the current-voltage curve of device with ITO/ETL/Au structure was measured to evaluate the electrical properties of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film (Figure  4e).[6] According to the results calculated by the equation $\mathbf{\Phi}^{\prime}(\sigma=I d(V A)^{-1}$ , where $A$ and $d$ are the area and thickness of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer, respectively), the vertical conductivity of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ after POC treatment boosted by $90\%$ from $2.12\times10^{3}$ to $4.04\times10^{3}\mathrm{mS\cm^{-1}}$ . This improved electrical properties of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ could be attributed to the manipulation of surface oxygen vacancies and enhanced electron transport via POC addition.
To further explore the charge carrier dynamics at the buried interface, steady–state and time-resolved PL were performed on $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /perovskite sample. As displayed in Figure  4f, the POC-tailoring sample exhibited more pronounced PL quenching compared to the control sample. Such PL quenching is indicative of more efficient carrier extraction. Fitted by a double exponential decay function, the estimated carrier extraction lifetimes for the control and target samples are 333.8 and $222.2~\mathrm{ns}$ , respectively (Figure 4g). The reduced carrier lifetime coincides with the significant PL quenching, together illustrating that POC tailoring can effectively improve carrier extraction and transport through chemical stitch sandwiched between $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and perovskite.[35] Transient photocurrent spectra (TPC) and transient photovoltage spectra (TPV) were measured to further examine transient carrier dynamics at the device level (Figure  4h,i). Compared with the control device, the TPC lifetime of the target device was significantly shortened (from 1430 to $619~\mathrm{ns}^{\prime}$ ), while the TPV lifetime was obviously increased (from 352 to $523~{\upmu\mathrm{s}})$ ), exhibiting greater charge extraction and suppressed charge recombination.
## 2.4. Suppressed Ion Migration Toward ETL and ITO
Due to the low migration activation energy $(0.1\ \mathrm{eV})$ , iodide ions can easily migrate from the perovskite bulk through vacancy defects to the grain boundaries and interfaces with high defect density.[37] Whereas, investigations on ion migration toward buried interface or even through ETL into ITO electrode still lacks systematic exploration.[26,41] Here we designed a series of experiments to characterize the possible effects of ion migration toward these downward interfaces. First, ToFSIMS was applied to observe the distribution of iodine in the control device after working for a period of time (Figure  S12,
Supporting Information). It was found that iodide ions can indeed migrate through ETL and accumulate on the ITO side (Figure  5a). To further confirm that iodide ions could migrate to ITO interface, we fabricated PSCs without ETL and characterized the exposed ITO surface via XPS by cleaning up the perovskite and above layers after $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ of device MPP operation (detailed device and characterization process is shown in Figure S13, Supporting Information). Here device MPP operation is applied to purposely simulate real working condition for solar cells for characterization. As shown in Figure  5b, iodine elements were surprisingly found on the surface of ITO after MPP operation, while not present in the pristine ITO. These results strongly suggest that iodine ions would migrate to ITO electrode under illumination and electric field.
The quite impressive finding leads us to investigate more on the effect of ion migration to the ITO interface. UPS measurement (Figure 5c) reveals that such migration leads to a change of surface work function of ITO after device MPP operation, from 4.55 to $4.73\:\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S14, Supporting Information). This increase in surface work function of ITO will enlarge the energy gap between ITO and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , which will lead to a decreased open circuit voltage of solar cell device.[52] Note this is the first report on detailed investigation to directly reveal the work function change of ITO surface caused by downward migrating ions and its detrimental effect on device Voc. Accordingly, it is particularly critical to suppress the ion migration at the substrate interface (interface between ITO and ETL) in order to improve the photovoltaic performance of PSCs.
Surprisingly, by POC tailoring the buried interface, the content of iodide ions migrated to the ITO side was significantly reduced by about an order of magnitude in the POC-device, demonstrated in SIMS result (Figure 5d). XPS result also demonstrates a remarkably decreased I 3d signals in POC treated device after the same $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ MPP operation (Figure  S15, Supporting Information). These results strongly imply a substantially suppressed ion migration toward ITO after POC addition. As host molecule, POC can specifically capture guest molecules such as iodide ions through electrostatic attraction, hydrogen bonding, and hydrophobic interactions, also known as hostguest interactions.[42] The host-guest interaction is further verified by a designed experiment by adding POC to $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in KI water solution and filtering. As Figure  5e depicts, the UV–vis absorption spectra of the filtrate showed that the iodine was completely removed and the color of the filtrate disappeared, confirming the host-guest effect of POC on iodine.[43] This effect will greatly help to suppress iodine ion migration through ETL and ITO (Figure  S16, Supporting Information). Moreover, the rectification effect test in the dark state shows that the reverse breakdown voltage of the POC-device was $-5.9~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ while that of the control device was $-2.1\mathrm{V}$ (Figure S17, Supporting Information). Since breakdown voltage is inversely correlated with ion migration, the reduced breakdown voltage implies suppressed mobile ions in POC-device.[53] The suppressed ion migration also leads to the much-weakened current-voltage hysteresis in POC ETL-free devices (Figure  S18 and Table  S3, Supporting Information). The detrimental issue on device $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ caused by ITO work function change mentioned above is well resolved as well by showing a dramatically improved $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ (1.01 to 1.06 V) in POC ETL-free devices as prototype devices.
To further verify the effectiveness of POC on inhibiting ion migration, in situ confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM) was exploited in Au/perovskite/Au lateral structure devices under the combined light illumination and electric field (Figure  5f ).[38,49] During optical imaging and tests, a $561~\mathrm{nm}$ laser and a fixed electric field $(2\mathrm{~V~}\upmu\mathrm{m}^{-1})$ were applied to simulate a harsh working state of solar cell operation. For the control device, severe degradation occurred near the black speck and rapidly expanded throughout the sample within 90  s (Figure  5g). The black speck produced by PL quenching is usually associated with ion migration, indicating a severe ion migration in control sample. On the distinct contrary, the PL images of the POC-device remained almost unchanged for 180  s (Figure  5h), demonstrating a more robust nature. Such enhanced inhibition of ion migration stems from the interaction of POC with iodide ions.[49]
## 2.5. Device Performance
To verify the tailoring effect of POC on solar cell devices, we examined the photovoltaic metrics of the planar PSCs with an n-i-p configuration of $\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}\mathrm{\mathrm{-}P O C/}}$ Perovskite/SpiroOMeTAD/Au. The $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ -based PSCs were fabricated via twostep deposition method. Figure  S19 (Supporting Information) displays the $J{-}V$ characteristics of PSCs with various concentrations of POC and the detailed photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table S4 (Supporting Information). Optimal devices were obtained with POC concentrations as low as $0.1\mathrm{\mm}$ indicating great potential for improving the economic viability of PSCs. Figure  6a depicted the $J{-}V$ curves of the champion devices under AM $1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ standard light irradiation (The hysteresis of the device is shown in Figure  S20 and Table  S5, Supporting Information). The PCE of the best-performing device is $24.13\%$ , with an open-circuit voltage (Voc) of 1.17 V, short-circuit current density $(J{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $25.54~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and fill factor (FF) of $80.76\%$ , which is manifestly better than that of the control device $(22.7\%)$ . More impressively, this is also currently the highest PCE of PSCs incorporated with organic porous materials (including MOFs and COFs, etc.) (Figure 6b and Table S6, Supporting Information). Obviously, the improvement of device performance is mainly contributed by the remarkably increased $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . Thanks to the spontaneous restructuring induced by POC at the buried interface, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ prominently enhanced by $40~\mathrm{mV}$ from 1.13 to 1.17 V, with an ultra-small $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss of only $0.39~\mathrm{V}.$ Moreover, the FF has also been improved from 0.794 to 0.806. The promoted Voc and FF are ascribed to the suppressed defects at restructured buried interface and the restrained mobile ions through POC chelation. In addition, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of the PSCs with only SDH tailoring the buried interface was also enhanced, but the performance was inferior to that of the POC-modified devices (Figure S21, Supporting Information). Therefore, the tri-dentate O-N-O chelating site with the ability to complex with metal ions provided by the POC is more favorable for the restructuring of the buried interface.
Steady current density outputs for 300  s measured at maximum power point (MPP) is shown in Figure S22 (Supporting Information). The $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the two devices obtained from the $J{-}V$ curves were nearly consistent, coinciding well with the integrating $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values confirmed by the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra (Figure S23, Supporting Information). The statistical photovoltaic parameters of control and target devices are demonstrated in Figure  6c,d and Figure  S24 (Supporting
Information). Through the average data of ${\approx}40$ devices, we found that the average $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of the control device and the target device are 1.11 and 1.16 V, respectively, with a remarkable improvement of $50~\mathrm{mV}.$ The universality and superiority of POC restructuring strategy was further demonstrated in PSC devices with $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}$ component. As shown in Figure S25 (Supporting Information), the PCE of the POC-restructured champion device reaches $19.99\%$ , with enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.12~\mathrm{V},$ $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $23.7\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $75.3\%$ . Such value is $12\%$ higher than that of the control device $(17.81\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of 1.06 V) and a remarkable increase in Voc by $60~\mathrm{mV}.$ Furthermore, PSCs with enlarged active area of $1{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ was fabricated to demonstrate the scalability of the POC restructuring strategy, exhibiting a PCE as high as $21.19\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.13\mathrm{V},J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $24.1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and FF of $77.8\%$ (Figure 6e).
Long term stability tests of PSCs under real atmospheric conditions were finally carried out. For the first $3000\mathrm{~h~}$ , the unencapsulated devices were stored in dry air. As shown in Figure  6f, the performance of the control device deteriorated substantially after $3000\mathrm{h}$ of aging, maintaining average $80\%$ of its initial PCE. In contrast, the target device remained almost unchanged at average $97\%$ of the initial value in the same period. For the following $2000{\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the devices were placed in a harsher environment under $25\%$ relative humidity. Remarkably, the POC-treated device still retained excellent stability at average $93\%$ of the initial value in a humidity environment, far exceeding that of the control device. Such result may be attributed to the hydrophobicity of calixarene in POC, which could be verified by the weakened hydrophilicity of POC- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface by contact angle measurement (Figure  S26, Supporting Information).[35] Additionally, the operational stability of the unencapsulated devices under continuous illumination ( $\mathrm{100~mW~cm^{-2}}$ , white LED) was evaluated under MPP tracking $(\mathrm{RH}{\sim}50\%$ without temperature control in summer). After $300\mathrm{min}$ of continuous testing, the output PCE of the control device dropped significantly to $18\%$ , while the target device sustained at over $80\%$ of the initial PCE after $800\ \mathrm{min}$ (Figure  S27, Supporting Information). The target device exhibited robust stability under different conditions, ascribed to the inhibited ion migration by POC on the spontaneously restructured buried interface and even the substrate interface.
## 3. Conclusion
In conclusion, POC was applied to restructure the buried interface of n-i-p PSCs by spontaneous forming coordination interaction with both $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in perovskite. POCs show  unique feature of total compatibility with PSC manufacturing process owing to its solution processability. These organic cages not only offer polydentate chelation sites for restructuring buried interface to lessen nonradiative recombination, but also contribute to the enhanced electricity conductivity and charge extraction at the interface between ETL and perovskite. More importantly, downward migrating of iodide ions toward ETL and even ITO has been deeply investigated through a series of designed experiments. It is revealed that such migration would invade the substrate interface (interface between ETL and ITO), leading to the change of ITO surface work function and even worsening Voc of PSCs. Remarkably, POC can specifically capture iodide ions to resist ion migration toward the buried and substrate interface through host-guest interactions. As a result, the fabricated solar cell device delivered a boosted PCE of $24.13\%$ with drastically enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . Moreover, the unencapsulated POC-restructured device retains $93\%$ of initial PCE after aging for over $5000\mathrm{{h}}$ in ambient condition. This investigation demonstrated the unique value of POC in successful tailoring buried interface in terms of decreasing defect density and suppressing ion migration. This work also highlights the importance of buried interface even substrate interface, and the significance on the necessity of suppressing ion migration toward these interfaces in improving both the efficiency and stability of perovskite photovoltaics.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), isopropanol (IPA), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), chlorobenzene (CB), lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ beads) was purchased from Sigma–Aldrich. $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ colloid precursor (tin (iv) oxide, $15\%$ in $H_{2}O$ colloidal dispersion) was received from Alfa Aesar. Acetonitrile (ACN) was obtained from TCI. Formamidinium iodide (FAI), methylammonium chloride (MACl), methylammonium iodide (MAI), p-F-PEAI (4-Fluoro-phenethylammonium iodide), lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide (Li-TFSI), 4-tert-butylpyridine (4-tBP) and spiro-OMeTAD were obtained from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology in China. [6,6]-Phenyl-C61-butyric Acid Methyl Ester $(P C_{67}\mathsf{B M})$ , $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ Indium tin oxide (ITO) were purchased from Advanced Election Technology Co., Ltd. Details of the chemical synthesis method of POC (HPOC-102) were referenced in the literature.[42]
Device Fabrication: The high-transparent ITO glass was cleaned by sequentially washing with glass cleaning liquid, deionized water, acetone and IPA. Before use, the ITO-coated glass was treated with $90\ \mathsf{W}$ plasma for $90\varsigma$ to improve wettability. The $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ precursor $(2.67\%$ , diluted with water) was spin-coated on the ITO at 3,000  rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and annealed at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $25\mathrm{\min}$ . For the target PSCs, the diluted POC (HPOC-102) solution (in DMF) was spin-coated on the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ layer at 5000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , and followed with a thermal treatment at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 5  min to remove the solvent and form a thin layer. Subsequently, the $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite layer was prepared by a two-step spin coating method in ${\sf N}_{2}$ glove box. First, $\mathsf{1}.5\mathrm{~\mathsf{M}~}\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ in DMF: DMSO (9:1) was deposited on the substrate at 1500 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , then annealed at $70~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ ; Second, the FAI: MACl: MAI $(90~\mathsf{m g};\mathsf{9}~\mathsf{m g};\mathsf{6.4~\mathsf{m g}}$ in $\mathsf{l m}|\mathsf{\Omega}|{\mathsf{P A}}\rangle$ was spin-coated at $2000~\mathsf{r p m}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , followed with annealing at ${150^{\circ}C}$ for 15  min in ambient air $(30\%-40\%$ humidity). For the $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ device, the $\mathsf{1}.5\mathrm{~M~}\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ solution was spin coated at 1800 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , then dried at $70^{\circ}C$ ; the MAI: MACl (50 mg: $\boldsymbol{{10.67}\ m g}$ in $\rceil\:m\!$ IPA) was spin-coated at 2500 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , followed with annealing at $135^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 12  min. Then, the $\mathsf{p}$ -F-PEAI solution dissolved in IPA with $2.5~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ was spin-coated onto the perovskite surface at 5,000 rpm without annealing. The spiroOMeTAD solution $[72.3\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ in CB with the addition of $77.5\upmu\mathrm{L}m\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ Li-TFSI/acetonitrile $(520\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})$ ) and $30~\upmu\mathrm{L}~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ 4-tBP] was coated at 4000  rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . This process was all carried out in ${\sf N}_{2}$ glove box except for annealing. At last, Au electrode (around $60\ \mathsf{n m}$ , $0.{\overset{\cdot}{1}}-0.3{\overset{\cdot}{\mathsf{A}}}/{\mathsf{s}})$ was deposited by thermal evaporation at a pressure of $8\times10^{-5}$ Torr.
Characterization: AFM images were obtained from Hitachi AFM5100W. XPS was measured using a Thermo Fisher Scientific ESCALAB $\mathsf{X i+}$ and the X-ray source is $(\mathsf{A l}\mathsf{K}\alpha,7486.6\mathsf{e V})$ . Especially the C 1s peak position at 284.8 eV was used for calibration. NMR measurement was examined on Bruker AVANCE III HD ( $(600~\mathsf{M H z}$ , DMSO-d6, $\delta)$ . The UPS measurement was conducted on Thermo Scientific NEXSA with a He I light source (21.2  eV). The vacuum of the chamber was $5\times10^{-9}$ mbar, and the bias voltage was -5  V. UV–vis spectra were performed on Shimadzu UV3600 plus. The ToF-SIMS was performed with ION ToF-SIMS 5–100 system, the voltage used for sputtering was 1  V $({\mathsf{B i}}^{3+})$ . Contact angle test was conducted on kruss DSA100. The above characterization services were provided by Ceshigo. The crystal structure and phase composition were determined by the X-Pert3 Powder (Holland) X-ray diffract meter with $\mathsf{C u}$ Ka1 radiation $(\lambda=1.5406\mathring{\mathsf{A}}_{\iota}^{}$ . FTIR measurement was examined on Thermo Fisher nicolet was 50. The SEM images were got from FEI Nova Nano SEM 430. PL and TRPL spectra were carried on Edinburgh Instruments Ltd. FLS980 fluorescence spectrometer system, and the excitation light was incident from the direction of the perovskite film. The characterization of ion migration under light and electric field were performed on a homemade Olympus FV3000 CLSM system. Lateral devices were fabricated by depositing gold electrodes (electrode spacing $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}};$ on perovskite films. It should be noted that POC was doped into perovskite in order to observe the effect of POC on ion migration more intuitively. The perovskite films were excited by a 561 nm continuous-wave laser.
The $J-V$ characterization were performed using an Agilent B2900 source measure unit under AM $\mathsf{1.5~C}$ irradiation $(700\ m\times100^{-2})$ with a Xeon lamp solar simulator (Solar IV-150A, Zolix). The light intensity was calibrated with a Newport calibrated KG5-filtered Si cell. The $J{-}V$ curves were measured with a scanning rate of voltage interval of $10~\mathsf{m v}$ and delay time of $200~\mathsf{m s}$ from 1.20 to $-0.01\mathrm{~V~}$ (reverse) or from $-0.01$ to $\ensuremath{\mathsf{1.20~v}}$ (forward). The devices were taken out for EQE measurement by a solar cell QE/IPCE measurement system (Solar Cell Scan100, Zolix) calibrated by a certified silicon solar cell. No bias light was used and the scan step was $5\ n m$ . The light stability test was measured under AM $\mathsf{1.5~C}$ white-LED solar simulator (VeraSol-2, AAA class, Newport) without UV part in glove box. The TPC and TPV spectra were measured using a homemade system by Prof. Qingbo Meng’s group in Institute of Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences. The EIS was conducted on a potentiostat/galvanostat (SP-150, Bio-Logic) at different biases. The hot plate (JW-600DG) for annealing was bought from Wuhan Junwei Technology Co., Ltd. The glue dispenser (REESEEN PvS mini7) used in the manufacture of perovskite film are from Jiangyin J. Wanjia Technology Co., Ltd.
